 "result": "I wrote the Dockerfile and two supporting files. I could not build or run the image because Bash is disabled in this session, so the build is unverified. I checked the Dockerfile against the source by reading it.\n\nFiles created in the repository root:\n\n- **`Dockerfile`**: two stages. The build stage uses `rust:1-bookworm` and runs `cargo build --release --locked`. The runtime stage uses `debian:bookworm-slim`, runs as a non-root user (uid 10001), listens on port 5001, and starts the `qiluo` binary.\n- **`config/docker.yaml`**: a copy of `config/development.yaml` that reads `DATABASE_URL`, `JWT_SECRET`, `SMTP_USER` and `SMTP_PASSWORD` from environment variables. The Dockerfile sets `environment=docker` to select it.\n- **`.dockerignore`**: excludes `target/`, `.git`, `.env` (it holds a DB password), runtime data under `data/`, and the Docker files themselves.\n\nNo existing files were changed.\n\nThings to know before running it:\n\n- **Database**: the default `DATABASE_URL` points at `127.0.0.1`, which is the container itself. Pass a reachable URL with `-e DATABASE_URL=...`. The app does not create the schema, so import `qiluo.sql` first.\n- **Secrets**: the JWT and SMTP defaults come from the existing development config. Set `JWT_SECRET` and the SMTP variables for any real deployment.\n- **Codegen**: the code generator writes into `src/model`, which is not in the image, so its output will not persist in the container.\n- **Untested**: I did not verify that the build succeeds or that the app starts. If the build fails, the likely causes are a Rust toolchain or native-dependency issue in the builder stage.\n\nSuggested commands, once you can run Docker:\n\n```\ndocker build -t qiluo .\ndocker run --rm -p 5001:5001 -e DATABASE_URL='mysql://user:pass@host.docker.internal:3306/qiluoopen' -e JWT_SECRET='...' qiluo\n```",
